{"tool_name":"goldrush","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"goldrush","home":"https://github.com/bcgsc/goldrush","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"Linear time complexity de novo long read genome assembly with GoldRush.","addition_date":"2024-01-10T11:19:02.919528Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T14:22:30.989898Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool"],"biotools_curie":"biotools:goldrush","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"ibirol@bcgsc.ca","name":"Inan\u00e7 Birol","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0003-0950-7839","typeEntity":"Person"},{"email":"jowong@bcgsc.ca","name":"Johnathan Wong","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1687-8972","typeEntity":"Person"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"public"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"De-novo assembly","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0524"},{"term":"Genome assembly","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0525"},{"term":"Scaffolding","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3216"},{"term":"Sequence trimming","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3192"},{"term":"de Novo sequencing","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3644"}]}],"name":"GoldRush","operating_system":["Linux"],"owner":"Pub2Tools","publication":[{"doi":"10.1038/s41467-023-38716-x","metadata":{"abstract":"Current state-of-the-art de novo long read genome assemblers follow the Overlap-Layout-Consensus paradigm. While read-to-read overlap \u2013 its most costly step \u2013 was improved in modern long read genome assemblers, these tools still often require excessive RAM when assembling a typical human dataset. Our work departs from this paradigm, foregoing all-vs-all sequence alignments in favor of a dynamic data structure implemented in GoldRush, a de novo long read genome assembly algorithm with linear time complexity. We tested GoldRush on Oxford Nanopore Technologies long sequencing read datasets with different base error profiles sourced from three human cell lines, rice, and tomato. Here, we show that GoldRush achieves assembly scaffold NGA50 lengths of 18.3-22.2, 0.3 and 2.6 Mbp, for the genomes of human, rice, and tomato, respectively, and assembles each genome within a day, using at most 54.5 GB of random-access memory, demonstrating the scalability of our genome assembly paradigm and its implementation.","authors":[{"name":"Birol I."},{"name":"Coombe L."},{"name":"Nikolic V."},{"name":"Nip K.M."},{"name":"Sidhu P."},{"name":"Warren R.L."},{"name":"Wong J."},{"name":"Zhang E."}],"citationCount":9,"date":"2023-12-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Nature Communications","title":"Linear time complexity de novo long read genome assembly with GoldRush"},"pmcid":"PMC10202940","pmid":"37217507","type":["Primary"]}],"topic":[{"term":"Plant biology","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0780"},{"term":"Sequence assembly","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0196"},{"term":"Whole genome sequencing","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3673"}]},"bioschemas":{"name":"GoldRush","home":"https://bio.tools/goldrush","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"Linear time complexity de novo long read genome assembly with GoldRush.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:goldrush/cmd/github.com","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"sourcecode":{"interpreted":false}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"license":{"open_source":true,"osi":true},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":20,"citations":[{"count":3,"year":"2026"},{"count":4,"year":"2024"},{"count":5,"year":"2023"},{"count":8,"year":"2025"}],"doi":"10.1038/s41467-023-38716-x","pmcid":"PMC10202940","pmid":"37217507","ref_count":49,"refs":[{"count":1,"year":"2002"},{"count":1,"year":"2006"},{"count":1,"year":"2012"},{"count":1,"year":"2024"},{"count":2,"year":"1998"},{"count":2,"year":"2015"},{"count":2,"year":"2016"},{"count":2,"year":"2017"},{"count":3,"year":"2011"},